.policy "alembic-practice-drop-mapped-column" revision 1
.description "Don't drop or rename a column or table in an Alembic upgrade while a model of the same migration tree still maps it; its queries fail once the upgrade runs."
.language python
.mode steer
.scope include "**/*.py"
// Rerail's own rule, not a port. What it checks: the migration and the models disagree. An upgrade drops or renames a
// column (or table) that a model of the application this Alembic tree migrates still maps, so every SELECT and INSERT
// of that model names a column that is gone ("column users.nick does not exist") once the upgrade runs, whatever the
// deploy order: the models must lose the attribute too. When migrations run before the new code is live (a prestart
// script, a release job), the attribute must go in an earlier release (expand/contract: strong_migrations "Removing a
// column"/"Renaming a column", Squawk ban-drop-column and renaming-column, GoCardless "Zero-downtime Postgres
// migrations: the hard parts", django-migration-linter DROP_COLUMN/RENAME_COLUMN; polar's env.py keeps
// UNMAPPED_COLUMNS for exactly this window). The rule cannot see the deploy order, so it only says when that matters.
// Reported, in `upgrade()` (or a module-level helper it calls) of a migration outside test code:
//   op.drop_column("users", "nick")                                 reported while a model of the tree maps users.nick
//   batch_op.drop_column("nick")                                    reported (table from batch_alter_table("users"))
//   op.alter_column("users", "nick", new_column_name="handle")      reported while a model of the tree maps users.nick
//   op.drop_table("users") / op.rename_table("users", "people")     reported while a model of the tree maps users
// The tree's models. A repository can hold several Alembic trees (a monorepo of services, each with its own database
// and env.py), so a migration sees only the models of the application its env.py migrates:
//   - the modules env.py imports, and theirs, transitively (`import a.b`, `from a import b` for a submodule b, relative
//     imports; importing a.b.c runs the packages a and a.b too), wherever they live: `from ledger.registry import
//     metadata` reaches `shared_models/entries.py` when registry imports it;
//   - every module of the same package (directory) as a module env.py's imports reach directly: `from app.models
//     import Base` with an `app/models/__init__.py` that imports only the base still covers `app/models/user.py`;
//   - when that finds no model (env.py imports no model, or only through `current_app` or a library), the modules
//     under env.py's top-level directory: `accounts/migrations/env.py` sees `accounts/**`, never `billing/models.py`.
//   A migration under no env.py (a run without it) sees every application model.
// A model maps users.nick when an application class (not in a migration, env.py or test file) of the tree has
// `__tablename__ = "users"` (or is a sqlmodel `table=True` class named User) and declares nick itself or on a mixin or
// base in another module: `nick = Column(...)`, `nick: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(...)`, a bare `nick: Mapped[str]`,
// a sqlmodel field, `Column("nick", ...)` / `Column(..., name="nick")` under another attribute, or a Column wrapped in
// `deferred(...)` / `column_property(...)`. A `Table("users", metadata, Column("nick", ...))` of the tree maps them
// too: an association table (Core queries name all its columns), or the `__table__` of a class (inline, a module
// name, or a name imported from another module), whose mapper decides.
// Not mapped (honoured as SQLAlchemy reads them):
//   __mapper_args__ = {"exclude_properties": ["bio"]}               bio (also a tuple or set, `dict(...)`, or a Column
//                                                                     object such as `prefs_table.c.theme`)
//   __mapper_args__ = {"include_properties": ["id", "token"]}       every column but id and token
//     (a class's own __mapper_args__, else the nearest one among its bases: Python's attribute lookup)
//   class User(TokenMixin, Base): legacy_token = None             the model unmaps the mixin's column (polar's way)
// Left alone:
//   op.drop_column("users", "old_avatar")                           no model of the tree maps it: the normal end state
//   op.drop_column("users", "nick") in accounts/migrations          only billing's models (another tree) map it
//   op.drop_column("users", "nick")                                 when a later operation of the same upgrade()
//   op.add_column("users", sa.Column("nick", sa.Text()))              adds it back (add_column, create_table, or an
//                                                                     alter_column/rename_table to that name): a rebuild
//   a historical revision whose descendant revision (by down_revision) adds the column or table back
//   op.drop_table("tags") in both upgrade() and downgrade()       a reset: something else (create_all) rebuilds it
//   downgrade(); migrations and models in test files; a model class declared inside a migration file
// Gaps:
//   - The attribute removed from the model in the same change as the drop: the models and the migration agree, so the
//     rule is silent, yet old processes still query the column if migrations run before the new code is live. The
//     rule cannot see the previous code or the deploy order (`alembic check` cannot either).
//   - Flask-SQLAlchemy `class Widget(db.Model)` without `__tablename__` (a generated table name), a `__tablename__`
//     computed by `declared_attr` (dispatch derives names from class names), imperative mappings
//     (`registry.map_imperatively`, `mapper(..., exclude_properties=...)`), `__mapper_args__` computed by a
//     `declared_attr`, tables named by a non-literal expression, `schema=` (a model of another schema with the same
//     table name still counts), columns a model maps under `__table_args__` or by reflection.
//   - A `__table__` Table that Core queries also read directly: its columns count only as its class maps them.
//   - Models the application imports that env.py's imports never reach, in a package other than theirs (silent):
//     dispatch's `data/source/models.py`, imported by `dispatch/__init__.py` but in a directory without
//     `__init__.py`, is the one model module of its 15 that its trees do not reach.
//   - An env.py that reads the context only as `alembic.context.configure(...)` after `import alembic.context`
//     (open-webui) is no env.py to lib/alembic's al_env_doc, so its migrations see every application model.
// Corpus (8 Alembic repositories, 2026-11-25): 0 matches, as expected for repositories whose migrations and models
// agree. Tree resolution there: the template, jupyterhub, mealie (through `_all_models`' star imports), polar, dify
// and dispatch reach their models through env.py's imports; superset (metadata through flask_appbuilder) falls back
// to `superset/`; open-webui has no recognised env.py (above). The edit-window copy of the template reports a new
// revision's drop of user.full_name, still mapped through UserBase, and stays silent on a drop of an unmapped column.

#include "lib/alembic.rerail"

.violation DropMappedColumn(at: node, t: symbol, x: symbol) "A model still maps {t}.{x}, which this upgrade drops. Remove the attribute, or keep the column if code needs it; ship the model change first if migrations run before new code is live."
.violation RenameMappedColumn(at: node, t: symbol, x: symbol, y: symbol) "A model still maps {t}.{x}, which this upgrade renames to {y}. Map {y} instead; if migrations run before new code is live, add {y} and backfill first, then drop {x} later."
.violation DropMappedTable(at: node, t: symbol) "A model still maps table {t}, which this upgrade drops. Remove the model and its relationships, or keep the table; ship that first if migrations run before new code is live."
.violation RenameMappedTable(at: node, t: symbol, y: symbol) "A model still maps table {t}, which this upgrade renames to {y}. Map {y} instead; if migrations run before new code is live, keep a view named {t} until old code is gone."

// ---- the models of each Alembic tree -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// dm_app_doc(d): application code: no migration, env.py or test file (a migration's own throwaway model is not the
// application's).
.decl dm_app_doc(d: number)
dm_app_doc(d) :- doc(d, _, _), !al_migration_doc(d), !al_env_doc(d), !al_test_doc(d).
// dm_import_mod(d, q): an import statement of document d names module q: `import q`, `from q import x`, `from q
// import *` (mealie's `_all_models`), and `from m import x` names q = m.x too (x may be a submodule). Relative imports
// arrive resolved (python/imports).
.decl dm_import_mod(d: number, q: symbol)
dm_import_mod(d, m) :- module_text(i, m), docof(i, d).
dm_import_mod(d, m) :- import_alias(i, _, m, _, _), docof(i, d).
dm_import_mod(d, @dot(m, x)) :- import_alias(i, _, m, x, _), x != "", docof(i, d).
// dm_import_pkg(d, p): importing q runs each package above it first (`import a.b.c` runs a and a.b).
.decl dm_import_pkg(d: number, p: symbol)
dm_import_pkg(d, p) :- dm_import_mod(d, q), p = @pkg_up(q, 1), p != "".
dm_import_pkg(d, p) :- dm_import_pkg(d, q), p = @pkg_up(q, 1), p != "".
// dm_imports(d, d2): document d imports the module of document d2 itself; dm_runs(d, d2): importing makes d2 run (d2
// itself, or a package above it).
.decl dm_imports(d: number, d2: number)
dm_imports(d, d2) :- dm_import_mod(d, q), doc(d2, _, q), d2 != d.
.decl dm_runs(d: number, d2: number)
dm_runs(d, d2) :- dm_imports(d, d2).
dm_runs(d, d2) :- dm_import_pkg(d, q), doc(d2, _, q), d2 != d.
// dm_reach(e, d): running env.py e runs module d: through its imports, transitively. dm_reached(e, d): d is imported
// by name on the way (not only a package above a module), so d's package is the one its models live in.
.decl dm_reach(e: number, d: number)
dm_reach(e, d) :- al_env_doc(e), dm_runs(e, d).
dm_reach(e, d) :- dm_reach(e, m), dm_runs(m, d), d != e.
.decl dm_reached(e: number, d: number)
dm_reached(e, d) :- al_env_doc(e), dm_imports(e, d).
dm_reached(e, d) :- dm_reach(e, m), dm_imports(m, d), d != e.
// dm_dir(d, p): the directory of document d ("" at the root).
.decl dm_dir(d: number, p: symbol)
dm_dir(d, p) :- doc(d, f, _), p = @replace_all(cat("/", f), "/[^/]*$", "").
// dm_import_tree(e, d): document d holds models env.py e loads: a module it runs, or a module of the same package
// (directory, not its subpackages) as a module it imports by name. The root directory is no package.
.decl dm_import_tree(e: number, d: number)
dm_import_tree(e, d) :- dm_reach(e, d).
dm_import_tree(e, d) :- dm_reached(e, d1), dm_dir(d1, p), p != "", dm_dir(d, p).
// dm_model_doc(d): application document d declares a model or a Core Table.
.decl dm_model_doc(d: number)
dm_model_doc(d) :- al_model(k, _), docof(k, d), dm_app_doc(d).
dm_model_doc(d) :- dm_core_table(c, _), docof(c, d).
.decl dm_import_tree_models(e: number)
dm_import_tree_models(e) :- dm_import_tree(e, d), dm_model_doc(d).
// dm_top_dir(d, s): document d's top-level directory s ("" for a file at the root).
.decl dm_top_dir(d: number, s: symbol)
dm_top_dir(d, s) :- doc(d, f, _), contains("/", f), s = @replace_all(f, "/.*$", "").
dm_top_dir(d, "") :- doc(d, f, _), !contains("/", f).
// dm_tree_doc(e, d): application document d belongs to the tree of env.py e: what env.py loads, or, when that holds
// no model, what sits under env.py's top-level directory (every document, for an env.py at the root).
.decl dm_tree_doc(e: number, d: number)
dm_tree_doc(e, d) :- dm_import_tree(e, d), dm_app_doc(d).
dm_tree_doc(e, d) :- al_env_doc(e), !dm_import_tree_models(e), dm_top_dir(e, s), s != "", dm_top_dir(d, s),
    dm_app_doc(d).
dm_tree_doc(e, d) :- al_env_doc(e), !dm_import_tree_models(e), dm_top_dir(e, ""), dm_app_doc(d).

// ---- what a class maps -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Columns al_class_column does not see: a Column wrapped in deferred()/column_property(), and `name="x"` given as a
// keyword. dm_extra_column(k, x, a): statement a of class k's body maps column x. (Candidates for lib/alembic.)
.decl dm_wrapper_q(q: symbol)
dm_wrapper_q("sqlalchemy.orm.deferred"). dm_wrapper_q("sqlalchemy.orm.column_property").
.decl dm_extra_column(k: node, x: symbol, a: node)
dm_extra_column(k, x, a) :- al_class_stmt(k, a), field(a, "right", v), unparen(v, w), kind(w, "call"),
    field(w, "function", f), sym_of(f, q), dm_wrapper_q(q), call_arg(w, 0, "", e), unparen(e, col),
    al_column_call(col), al_column_name(col, x).
dm_extra_column(k, x, a) :- al_class_stmt(k, a), field(a, "right", v), unparen(v, w), kind(w, "call"),
    field(w, "function", f), sym_of(f, q), dm_wrapper_q(q), call_arg(w, 0, "", e), unparen(e, col),
    al_column_call(col), !al_column_name(col, _), field(a, "left", l), text(l, x).
dm_extra_column(k, x, a) :- al_class_stmt(k, a), field(a, "right", v), al_mapped_value(v), unparen(v, c),
    call_arg(c, -1, "name", e), al_name_value(e, x).
.decl dm_declared_column(k: node, x: symbol, a: node)
dm_declared_column(k, x, a) :- al_class_column(k, x, a).
dm_declared_column(k, x, a) :- dm_extra_column(k, x, a).
// dm_core_table(c, t): `Table("t", metadata, ...)` in application code: an association table, or a `__table__`.
.decl dm_table_q(q: symbol)
dm_table_q("sqlalchemy.Table"). dm_table_q("sqlalchemy.schema.Table"). dm_table_q("sqlalchemy.sql.schema.Table").
dm_table_q("sqlmodel.Table").
.decl dm_core_table(c: node, t: symbol)
dm_core_table(c, t) :- kind(c, "call"), field(c, "function", f), sym_of(f, q), dm_table_q(q), call_arg(c, 0, "", a),
    al_name_value(a, t), call_arg(c, 1, "", _), docof(c, d), dm_app_doc(d).
// dm_table_value(v, c): expression v is Table c: the call itself, a module-level name bound to it in the same file,
// or a name imported from the module that binds it.
.decl dm_table_value(v: node, c: node)
dm_table_value(v, c) :- unparen(v, c), dm_core_table(c, _).
dm_table_value(v, c) :- unparen(v, u), kind(u, "identifier"), text(u, x), docof(u, d), al_top_assign(d, x, r),
    unparen(r, c), dm_core_table(c, _).
dm_table_value(v, c) :- unparen(v, u), sym_of(u, q), al_top_assign(d2, x, r), unparen(r, c), dm_core_table(c, _),
    doc(d2, _, mq), q = @dot(mq, x).
// dm_table_class(k, c): class k maps Table c: `__table__ = <c>`.
.decl dm_table_class(k: node, c: node)
dm_table_class(k, c) :- al_class_stmt(k, a), field(a, "left", l), text(l, "__table__"), field(a, "right", v),
    dm_table_value(v, c).
// dm_super(k, b): class b of the run is among the bases of k, a model or a `__table__` class, directly or not.
.decl dm_super(k: node, b: node)
dm_super(k, b) :- al_model_super(k, b).
dm_super(k, b) :- dm_table_class(k, _), class_base(k, q), class_def(b, q).
dm_super(k, b) :- dm_table_class(k, _), dm_super(k, m), class_base(m, q), class_def(b, q).
// Mapper arguments. dm_mapper_seq(k, key, s): class k's own `__mapper_args__` (a dict display or `dict(...)`) gives
// key the sequence s; dm_mapper_name(k, key, x): s lists x, as a string or a Column object (`prefs_table.c.theme`).
.decl dm_mapper_args(k: node, v: node)
dm_mapper_args(k, v) :- al_class_stmt(k, a), field(a, "left", l), text(l, "__mapper_args__"), field(a, "right", r),
    unparen(r, v).
.decl dm_mapper_seq(k: node, key: symbol, s: node)
dm_mapper_seq(k, key, s) :- dm_mapper_args(k, v), kind(v, "dictionary"), child(v, p, _), kind(p, "pair"),
    field(p, "key", kn), unparen(kn, ks), str(ks, key), field(p, "value", s0), unparen(s0, s).
dm_mapper_seq(k, key, s) :- dm_mapper_args(k, v), kind(v, "call"), field(v, "function", f), text(f, "dict"),
    call_arg(v, -1, key, s0), unparen(s0, s).
.decl dm_seq_kind(s: symbol)
dm_seq_kind("list"). dm_seq_kind("tuple"). dm_seq_kind("set").
.decl dm_mapper_name(k: node, key: symbol, x: symbol)
dm_mapper_name(k, key, x) :- dm_mapper_seq(k, key, s), kind(s, sk), dm_seq_kind(sk), child(s, e, _), str(e, x).
dm_mapper_name(k, key, x) :- dm_mapper_seq(k, key, s), kind(s, sk), dm_seq_kind(sk), child(s, e, _),
    attr_of(e, _, x).
// dm_args_of(k, b): the `__mapper_args__` that applies to class k is b's: k's own, else one among its bases (Python
// finds the attribute on the nearest base; when several bases set it, each counts).
.decl dm_args_of(k: node, b: node)
dm_args_of(k, k) :- dm_mapper_args(k, _).
dm_args_of(k, b) :- dm_super(k, b), !dm_mapper_args(k, _), dm_mapper_args(b, _).
.decl dm_excluded(k: node, x: symbol)
dm_excluded(k, x) :- dm_args_of(k, b), dm_mapper_name(b, "exclude_properties", x).
.decl dm_includes(k: node)
dm_includes(k) :- dm_args_of(k, b), dm_mapper_seq(b, "include_properties", _).
.decl dm_included(k: node, x: symbol)
dm_included(k, x) :- dm_args_of(k, b), dm_mapper_name(b, "include_properties", x).
// dm_unmapped_attr(k, y): model k, or a class among its bases, sets attribute y to None: the way a subclass stops
// mapping a column a mixin declares (`client_secret = None`), while the column still exists in the database.
.decl dm_sets_none(k: node, y: symbol)
dm_sets_none(k, y) :- al_class_stmt(k, a), !field(a, "type", _), field(a, "left", l), text(l, y), field(a, "right", v),
    unparen(v, u), kind(u, "none").
.decl dm_unmapped_attr(k: node, y: symbol)
dm_unmapped_attr(k, y) :- al_model(k, _), dm_sets_none(k, y).
dm_unmapped_attr(k, y) :- al_model_super(k, b), dm_sets_none(b, y).
// dm_class_column(k, x, y): class k declares column x under attribute y (a Table's column: its own name), itself or
// on a class among its bases; dm_hidden(k, x, y): k's mapper leaves it out (excluded by column or attribute name, or
// named by neither in include_properties).
.decl dm_class_column(k: node, x: symbol, y: symbol)
dm_class_column(k, x, y) :- al_model(k, _), dm_declared_column(k, x, a), field(a, "left", l), text(l, y).
dm_class_column(k, x, y) :- al_model(k, _), al_model_super(k, b), dm_declared_column(b, x, a), field(a, "left", l),
    text(l, y).
dm_class_column(k, x, x) :- dm_table_class(k, c), call_arg(c, i, "", e), i > 1, unparen(e, col), al_column_call(col),
    al_column_name(col, x).
.decl dm_hidden(k: node, x: symbol, y: symbol)
dm_hidden(k, x, y) :- dm_class_column(k, x, y), dm_unmapped_attr(k, y).
dm_hidden(k, x, y) :- dm_class_column(k, x, y), dm_excluded(k, x).
dm_hidden(k, x, y) :- dm_class_column(k, x, y), dm_excluded(k, y).
dm_hidden(k, x, y) :- dm_class_column(k, x, y), dm_includes(k), !dm_included(k, x), !dm_included(k, y).
// dm_maps(d, t, x): application document d maps column x of table t: a model class there, or a Core Table that no
// class maps (a Core query names all its columns).
.decl dm_maps(d: number, t: symbol, x: symbol)
dm_maps(d, t, x) :- al_model(k, t), dm_class_column(k, x, y), !dm_hidden(k, x, y), docof(k, d), dm_app_doc(d).
dm_maps(d, t, x) :- dm_table_class(k, c), dm_core_table(c, t), dm_class_column(k, x, y), !dm_hidden(k, x, y),
    docof(k, d), dm_app_doc(d).
.decl dm_class_mapped_table(c: node)
dm_class_mapped_table(c) :- dm_table_class(_, c).
dm_maps(d, t, x) :- dm_core_table(c, t), !dm_class_mapped_table(c), call_arg(c, i, "", e), i > 1, unparen(e, col),
    al_column_call(col), al_column_name(col, x), docof(c, d).
.decl dm_maps_table(d: number, t: symbol)
dm_maps_table(d, t) :- al_model(k, t), docof(k, d), dm_app_doc(d).
dm_maps_table(d, t) :- dm_core_table(c, t), docof(c, d).
// dm_sees_column(m, t, x) / dm_sees_table(m, t): a model of migration m's tree maps t.x / t; with no env.py above m,
// any application model.
.decl dm_sees_column(m: number, t: symbol, x: symbol)
dm_sees_column(m, t, x) :- al_env_tree(m, e), dm_tree_doc(e, d), dm_maps(d, t, x).
dm_sees_column(m, t, x) :- al_migration_doc(m), !al_in_some_tree(m), dm_maps(_, t, x).
.decl dm_sees_table(m: number, t: symbol)
dm_sees_table(m, t) :- al_env_tree(m, e), dm_tree_doc(e, d), dm_maps_table(d, t).
dm_sees_table(m, t) :- al_migration_doc(m), !al_in_some_tree(m), dm_maps_table(_, t).

// ---- what an upgrade removes and adds ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// dm_upgrade_op(c, m, f): operation m (c) runs in upgrade step f of a migration outside test code.
.decl dm_upgrade_op(c: node, m: symbol, f: node)
dm_upgrade_op(c, m, f) :- al_call(c, m, _), al_in_step(c, f), al_step(f, "upgrade"), docof(c, d), !al_test_doc(d).
.decl dm_new_name(c: node, y: symbol)
dm_new_name(c, y) :- al_kw(c, "new_column_name", v), al_name_value(v, y).
.decl dm_adds_column(c: node, f: node, t: symbol, x: symbol)
dm_adds_column(c, f, t, x) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "add_column", f), al_table(c, t), al_added_column(c, col),
    al_column_name(col, x).
dm_adds_column(c, f, t, x) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "create_table", f), al_table(c, t), call_arg(c, i, "", e), i > 0,
    unparen(e, col), al_column_call(col), al_column_name(col, x).
dm_adds_column(c, f, t, x) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "alter_column", f), al_table(c, t), dm_new_name(c, x).
.decl dm_adds_table(c: node, f: node, t: symbol)
dm_adds_table(c, f, t) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "create_table", f), al_table(c, t).
dm_adds_table(c, f, t) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "rename_table", f), al_role(c, "new_table", e), al_name_value(e, t).

// ---- revision order ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// dm_later(m, n): revision n descends from revision m (lib/alembic's al_ancestor: its down_revision chain reaches m).
.decl dm_later(m: number, n: number)
dm_later(m, n) :- al_ancestor(n, m).
// dm_column_back(c, f, t, x): after operation c of step f, t.x exists again: a later operation of the same step, or
// any upgrade of a descendant revision, adds it back.
.decl dm_column_back(c: node, f: node, t: symbol, x: symbol)
dm_column_back(c, f, t, x) :- dm_adds_column(a, f, t, x), dm_upgrade_op(c, _, f), earlier(c, a).
dm_column_back(c, f, t, x) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, _, f), docof(c, m), dm_later(m, n), dm_adds_column(a, _, t, x),
    docof(a, n).
.decl dm_table_back(c: node, f: node, t: symbol)
dm_table_back(c, f, t) :- dm_adds_table(a, f, t), dm_upgrade_op(c, _, f), earlier(c, a).
dm_table_back(c, f, t) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, _, f), docof(c, m), dm_later(m, n), dm_adds_table(a, _, t), docof(a, n).
// dm_reset_table(f, t): the revision of upgrade f drops t in its downgrade() too: neither direction keeps the table,
// so something else builds it again (jupyterhub's 833da8570507 drops api_tokens both ways, and the hub's
// `create_all()` recreates it from the model at startup, with the hub stopped for `upgrade-db`). A removal writes
// a downgrade that restores the table instead.
.decl dm_reset_table(f: node, t: symbol)
dm_reset_table(f, t) :- al_step(f, "upgrade"), docof(f, d), al_call(c, "drop_table", "op"), al_in_step(c, g),
    al_step(g, "downgrade"), docof(g, d), al_table(c, t).

// ---- reports -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DropMappedColumn(c, t, x) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "drop_column", f), al_table(c, t), al_role(c, "column_name", e),
    al_name_value(e, x), docof(c, m), dm_sees_column(m, t, x), !dm_column_back(c, f, t, x).
RenameMappedColumn(c, t, x, y) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "alter_column", f), al_table(c, t), al_role(c, "column_name", e),
    al_name_value(e, x), dm_new_name(c, y), y != x, docof(c, m), dm_sees_column(m, t, x), !dm_column_back(c, f, t, x).
DropMappedTable(c, t) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "drop_table", f), al_table(c, t), docof(c, m), dm_sees_table(m, t),
    !dm_table_back(c, f, t), !dm_reset_table(f, t).
RenameMappedTable(c, t, y) :- dm_upgrade_op(c, "rename_table", f), al_table(c, t), al_role(c, "new_table", e),
    al_name_value(e, y), y != t, docof(c, m), dm_sees_table(m, t), !dm_table_back(c, f, t).

